column2:
Would that this word, stranger, may be fulfilled;
164	then you would soon know friendship and many gifts
165	from me, so that anyone meeting you would call you blessed.

Young men, since you have all delighted your minds with contests,
175	come to the house, that we may prepare the meal;
176	for it is no bad thing to take supper in due time.

Take this and give it to the stranger, and tell him
346	to beg, going round to all the suitors;
347	shame is not good for a man in need to have by him.

Telemachus gives you this, stranger, and bids you
351	beg, going round to all the suitors;
352	he says shame is not good for a beggar man.

Be silent, do not answer him with many words;
394	Antinous is accustomed to provoke badly always
395	with harsh words, and urges others too.

Nurse, all are hateful, since they devise evils;
500	but Antinous most of all is like black doom.
501	Some unlucky stranger wanders through the house
502	begging of the men; for want bids him;
503	there all the others filled his wallet and gave,
504	but this man hit him with a footstool on the right shoulder.

The stranger is not witless; he supposes how it would be;
587	for nowhere among mortal men are there men
588	who in insolence devise such reckless things.

So it shall be, father; and you go, having taken your evening meal;
600	and in the morning come and bring fair victims;
601	but all this will be the care of me and the immortals.
